 { Same position, knight's file (Lolli) However, Philidor's method does not work on the knight's file. This position is known as Lolli's Position and it is a draw. Since Black's moves are forced, which sometimes makes things easier, we can use it as a defensive resource when in trouble. It is very important to note that here, the defender should move the king towards the corner of the same colour as the bishop. You could think that this is a safe drawing method, and it is, actually, if you are a computer, but Black is on a knife edge, so I do not recommend it. In principle, you do not need to study this method. I will show you some lines with a few comments. } 1. Rd8+ Rc8 2. Rd7 Rc1 3. Rf7 Rc2 4. Rg7 { Waiting strategy leads to nothing, as there is no zugzwang. } Rc1 5. Ba4 $1 { The most dangerous move. } Rc3 ( 5... Rb1 { also draws, but the text is more illustrative for our purposes. } ) 
6. Bc6 Rb3+ $1 7. Bb5 Rc3 $1 8. Bc6 Rb3+ 9. Kc5 { The king heads for d6 and Black cannot stop him. } Rb1 { Other moves may also draw but, as we said, it is better to have a clear idea and the edge of the board is a good reference. } 10. Bd5 { If } ( 10. Kd6 { the only move is } Rf1 $1 { . } ) 
Rf1 $1 { Black has to be ready to deliver checks on the 6th rank. } 11. Kd6 { Building a sort of Philidor Position with the black king on b8 instead of d8. Another attempt would be } ( 11. Rb7 $5 Kc8 12. Re7 Kb8 $1 13. Kb6 Rc1 $1 14. Be6 Rb1 15. Kc6 Rc1 16. Kd6 Rh1 17. Bd5 Rh6 $11 ) 
Rf6+ $1 12. Be6 Rf1 13. Rg8+ Ka7 $1 14. Bd5 Rd1 15. Ra8+ Kb6 16. Rb8+ Ka7 17. Ra8+ Kb6 18. Ra3 Rb1 $11 { Reaching the second-rank defence (see Ending 96) . } 
