 { Chessable note: This variation appears as informational because it contains a mistake for Black on move 4 who is meant to be drawing. White also makes a mistake, therefore this variation is for your review only. We have split this into two learn-able variations, one showing the correct defensive procedure, also here as a subvariation, and another showing how to exploit the mistake. As we have stated, the Cochrane Defence works extremely well on the central files, and it is rather easy to achieve from a normal position. When the starting position of the king is more delicate, or when a careless defence makes it impossible to use the main defensive method, it is sometimes still possible to adopt the so-called second-rank defence. This is a simple and un-breakable defensive set-up, despite its somewhat worrying appearance. The second-rank defence consists in placing both rook and king on the second rank. When a check forces the king to the first rank, an immediate rook-swap offer emerges on the board, which allows the king to come back to the 2nd rank on the following move. The king may also be forced to the first rank by means of zugzwang, but then the defending side can hold thanks to stalemate resources.In the following position, the black king is already locked on the edge of the board. Though no concrete threats can be seen, he may end up in the Philidor Position if he is not careful. It is time to use the second-rank defence. } 1... Kh3 { Instead of playing neutral moves which may lead to dangerous positions, Black immediately seeks a defensive procedure. The idea is ... Rg2. } 2. Bf4 Rg2 3. Rh7+ Kg4 4. Ke4 { This is one of the basic positions of the second-rank defence. Although Black has apparently fallen into zugzwang, he has a simple way to hold. The fact that the situation is extreme can help us find our way, but even experienced players may go astray in this ending. } Rc2 $2 { A surprising mistake that allows White to reach the Philidor Position. The obvious way was correct: } ( 4... Re2 $1 5. Be3 Rg2 { and Black holds. For instance: } ( 5... Rc2 5. Rg7 Kh3 7. Bg5 Kg4 8. Rg8 Kg3 9. Bf4 Kh3 10. Rh8 Kg4 { and we have the same ­position with White to move, but White cannot cause trouble. } ) 
 ) 
5. Be3 $2 { White misses an opportunity to reach the Philidor Position. } ( 5. Rg7 Kh4 6. Kf5 Rc5 7. Be5 { and White’s set-up is lethal. } ) 
Rg2 { Black returns to the right way. } 6. Rh1 { Another interesting, but well-known, zugzwang attempt. Once again, despite the distressing appearance of Black’s position, he manages to draw by keeping both pieces on the second rank by means of only moves. } Kg3 $1 ( 6... Rg3 $11 ) 
7. Bf4+ Kf2 { Black shifts from the g-file to the genuine ‘second rank’, maybe because it is easier to record the moves on the score sheet... } ( 7... Kg4 $11 ) 
8. Rc1 Ke2 9. Rc3 Kf2 10. Ra3 Ke2 11. Bg3 $5 { This is the zugzwang position we mentioned in the introduction. Again, Black has only one method, but it is simple and safe. } Kf1 $1 ( 11... Kd1 $11 ) 
12. Rf3+ ( 12. Kf3 Rf2 $11 { is a well-known stalemate resource; White does not want to try. } ) 
Ke2 13. Re3+ Kd2 $1 { Now it would be wrong to play } ( 13... Kf1 $4 14. Re1 { . The threat is ...Re2, forcing an immediate draw. } ) 
14. Kd4 ( 14. Kf3 $6 Rxg3 $11 ) 
Kd1 { The king may move to the 1st rank as long as it is guaranteed that he will quickly return to the second rank. Anyway, it was not forced: } ( 14... Re2 $1 15. Ra3 Kc2 $11 ) 
15. Ra3 Ke2 16. Ke4 Kf1 17. Kf3 { At last White decides to try the stalemate resource. } Rf2+ $1 { The only move – but enough. } 18. Kg4 ( 18. Ke4 { would lead to a repetition. } ) 
Rc2 { Transferring the rook to the other side; almost every rook move on the 2nd rank draws: (18... Re2=;) } ( 18... Rd2 $11 { - but not } ( 18... Rg2 $2 19. Ra1 Ke2 20. Kh3 { and the rook is trapped. } ) 
 ) 
19. Re3 Re2 20. Rf3+ Kg2 21. Rf4 Ra2 22. Re4 Rc2 { The black rook always keeps an eye on the 2nd rank, while White is running out of ideas. } 23. Bd6 Kf1 { Black gets ready for second-rank checks. A waiting strategy was still possible: } ( 23... Ra2 $11 ) 
24. Bb4 Rg2+ 25. Kh3 Rc2 26. Ba5 Ra2 27. Bb6 $6 { White creates a stalemate theme, and Black uses it to drive the white king off and gain some moves. } Ra3+ 28. Kg4 Rg3+ 29. Kf4 Rf3+ 30. Ke5 Kg2 31. Re2+ Kg3 
