 { Pawn on the 5th rankWhen the pawn is on the 5th rank, the ending is still drawn, but it requires a more accurate defence. This has caused many strong players to go astray. See Position 13.22. The first important rule to know is something we could infer from the previous example: The bishop must occupy the diagonal where it can control the pawn's advance to the 7th rank. } 1. Ra7 Bd3 { Leaving the right diagonal with } ( 1... Bb3 $2 { is careless and it will be punished. However, this is a quite frequent mistake, the reason probably being the similarity between this position and the Rook vs. Bishop ending, which leads to the erroneous feeling that, as long as our bishop can cover the check, we are safe. } 2. Kh6 { . Now there is no time for the bishop to return to the saving diagonal: () A. } Kg8 { B. } ( 2... Bc4 3. Ra8 Bg8 4. Kg5 Kh7 ( 4... Kg7 5. h6 { is similar } ) 
5. h6 Bd5 6. Ra7 Kh8 7. Re7 $1 { the rook covers the square the bishop needs in order to return to the right diagonal, and at the same time avoids a well-known trap which only works when the rook is on the a7-square. This trap has occurred in some actual games: } ( 6... Bd3 6. h7 $2 Be4 8. Kh6 Bb7 $11 { and there is no way to win due to stalemate resources: } 9. Ra4 Be4 $1 ) 
Bg8 8. Kg6 Bc4 9. h7 $18 { followed by checkmate on the 8th rank. } ) 
3. Rg7 Kh8 ( 3... Kf8 4. Kg6 { followed by Kf6, h6, h7 and h8 } ) 
4. Rc7 { controlling the c2 check and threatening Kg6 } Ba2 5. Rc8 { leads to similar positions to the variation 2... Bc4 } ) 
2. Kh6 Kg8 $1 { Knowing that the bishop must not leave the b1-h7 diagonal, this odd-looking move is easy to find. If we ignore that rule, it is very easy to fall into the 'logical' defence mentioned in the previous note. } 3. Rg7+ { Analysis diagram 13.23 } ( 3. Ra8 Kf7 { and, though the black king has left the corner, his white counterpart cannot get in: } 4. Kg5 Bc2 5. h6 { . It is interesting to know that, even with this disposition of pieces, Black is able to draw. } ) 
Kf8 $1 { Again, this move is easy to find in view of } ( 3... Kh8 4. Rd7 $18 { , winning. Now the black king is out of the corner, but any white move would allow him back. } ) 
4. Rg3 { Intending to take the white king off the h-file, and preventing the black king from returning to the h8-square. However, this double purpose fails against an accurate defence. } Bc2 $1 ( 4... Bb1 { would also draw, but only these two moves work; Black should avoid the trap } ( 4... Be4 $2 { allowing the white king off the h-file: } 5. Kg5 $1 { +- } Kg8 $6 6. Kf4 { . } ) 
 ) 
5. Rg6 { Another attempt to trick Black, but there is no reason to capture the rook. } Bd3 6. Kh7 { Threatening Kh8, winning; the rook cannot be captured, but... } Kf7 $1 { The only move, and enough to draw. } 7. Kh6 Kf8 8. Rg1 ( 8. Kg5 Bxg6 $11 { . Here Black can capture the rook. } ) 
Bc2 9. Kg5 Kg8 $11 { White has already tried everything. } 
